# One Dog Short - a shepherd, a working dog losing his sight, and a whistle nobody else answers.
# Written for the library. One person, one animal that works, and a decision about the end of a partnership.

title: One Dog Short
start: start

== start ==
bg cliff_path
music room_tone
Nine hundred ewes on two hills and one dog, because the other one died in the February at eleven
years old and you did not replace him, and you have been telling yourself since the spring that
you will do it in the autumn.
Autumn is now, and the dog you have left is Moss, and Moss is nine and a half and going blind.
-> the_hill

== the_hill ==
You noticed it in the June and you were sure of it in the August, which is when he ran the
gather on the far side and came in wide on a gate that has been in the same place for forty
years, and stood there with his head up and his ears going, waiting for a whistle he had already
had.
His nose is perfect. His ears are perfect. He works the near hill exactly as he always has,
because he knows it, and knowing it is not the same as seeing it and it is doing the work.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
Three ways you could go about it and every shepherd on this valley has taken one of them:
You can work him on the near hill only and keep the far hill for yourself, which means the far
hill takes twice as long and you are fifty-eight.
You can get a pup now and train him alongside Moss, who will teach him the near hill and show
him what a whistle means, and Moss will spend his last working year being a teacher.
Or you can do what your father did, which is to take the dog out one morning and come back
without him, and never say which morning.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
Moss is lying by the gate with his chin on his paws and one ear turned at you, and the light is
going, and the far hill is where the ewes are that need moving.
* Near hill only, and take the far hill yourself. -> near_hill
* A pup now, and let Moss teach him. -> the_pup
* Ring the vet and ask what he thinks about the dog's sight. -> the_vet
* The way your father did it, on a morning you do not announce. -> ending_not_him

== near_hill ==
You work the near hill with him for another two seasons, and he is good at it until the end,
and the far hill takes you twice as long every single time, which costs you about three weeks a
year and a knee.
You never once say out loud that you are doing it for him. You say it is because the pup would
be more trouble than he is worth, which is a sentence you have said to four different people
including the man at the market, and which is not true, and everybody who knows you knows it is
not true.
-> ending_near

== the_pup ==
You get a bitch pup in the October from a man in the next valley and she is called Nell and she
is everything a pup is, which is to say she is a menace and she learns in a fortnight what it
took Moss a season.
Moss teaches her. That is not a figure of speech: he takes her out and corrects her and stands
over her at the gate, and by the March she is working the near hill with him and by the June she
is doing it better, and he follows her, and he does it with his head up and his ears going.
-> ending_the_pup

== the_vet ==
He comes out to the yard because he is a farm vet and that is what happens, and he looks at both
eyes in the light of the open back of his car and says the thing you already knew and then says
the thing you had not thought of.
vet: He's not in pain. He's not confused. He's a dog doing a job he can still do on ground he
knows, and the question you're actually asking me is whether you're allowed to keep working him.
-> the_allowed

== the_allowed ==
you: Am I.
vet: Nobody's ever asked me that before. It's your dog and your hill.
He says it without any weight at all, which is the professional thing, and then he adds one more
sentence while he is packing his bag, which is not professional and which is why you remember it.
vet: I'd not stop him while he's still enjoying it. You'll know. He'll tell you, and it won't
be with his voice.
-> END

== ending_not_him ==
You do not do it. That is the whole of this: you stand at the gate in the dark on a morning in
the November with the rifle in the truck and you do not do it, and you drive back to the house
and put the rifle away and make tea, and Moss gets up off the flagstones and comes and sits with
you, and neither of you ever mentions it because only one of you knows.
Your father did it that way because there was no money and no vet within eleven miles and a dog
that could not work was a mouth. None of those three things is true of you.
-> END

== ending_near ==
Two more seasons. He works the near hill until the end and he never once runs wide on a gate,
because the near hill is in his head and his head is fine.
The far hill takes you twice as long and you are fifty-eight and you do it anyway, and in the
last summer you take him up there with you twice, on the lead, just to be on it, and he stands
on the top with his nose going and his head up and he is entirely happy, and you tell him the
names of the four farms you can see, which is a thing you have not done since you were a boy and
which nobody was there to hear.
-> END

== ending_the_pup ==
By the second summer Nell is doing the far hill on her own whistle and Moss goes with her because
he wants to, and there are two dogs in the truck again and the seat is full of hair.
He goes in the January, at eleven, in his sleep on the flagstones, which is the ending every
shepherd wants and almost none of them get. Nell is on the far hill by then and she is better
than he was, and she is not the same, and you find that out over about four years, and you find
it in small things: she comes in tight where he came in wide, and she does not care about the
gate, and she answers the whistle the first time, every time, and there is a version of a
partnership where that is the whole of it, and it took you four years to stop thinking that.
-> END
